// the real resolved executable path, not `argv[0]`.
//
// `mode: build`, because that is where the question has an interesting answer: under `run` a JIT'd program
// is echoc's own process, so `exe()` truthfully reports the compiler. Here it is the linked binary.
//
// the path differs per machine, so what is asserted is its shape - absolute, non-empty, and *not* the
// relative path `argv[0]` carries, which is what makes this more than a restatement of arg(0).

std::fs::path $path = guard std::env::exe();

echo $path->view()->size > 0;
#[if: os == windows]
echo $path->view()->byte(1) == 58;
echo $path->string()->ends('exe');
#[else]
echo $path->is_absolute();
echo $path->string()->ends('exe');
#[end]
